as_exercise_controls"}]}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "audio", "title": "", "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n4le01ob03re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "0", "text": "<strong>Jacob:</strong> Hi, Jane. Do you have a minute?<br>\n<strong>Jane: </strong>  Hi, Jacob. Sure, no problem. How can I help you? <br>\n<strong>Jacob:</strong> I have problems with the history of software programming and its evolution. I used to know about it, but now I can't remember. Did you learn something about it back in school?</br>\n<strong>Jane:</strong> Actually, I did. Let's see... The first computer program was the Analytical Engine, it was designed by Charles Babbage who also introduced very functional and important computing concepts. Then, Hernan Hollerith provided an input and output using a very practical method: punched cards. At the beginning of the 20th century, the first computers had specialized codes for their applications. Numerical calculations were based on decimal numbers, which meant that logic was represented with both, number and words. <br>", "image": "enpe7n4le01ob03re2img01.jpg", "id": 0}, {"time": "54500", "text": "<strong>Jacob:</strong> Did you find information about the first programming languages? <br>\n<strong>Jane:</strong> Well, that's not precise. There is some information dated back in 1940 when computers were electrical and included limited speed and memory capacity. In the 1950's, the Germans invented a high-level programming language that communicated instructions to computers. This decade was considered a starting point where the first steps appeared for programming language.  <br>", "image": "enpe7n4le01ob03re2img02.jpg", "id": 1}, {"time": "86000", "text": "<strong>Jacob:</strong> So, did this period establish the fundamental paradigms for programming languages?<br>\n<strong>Jane: </strong> Not exactly. The 60s and the 70s brought a major flowering in the field of programming languages. For example, most of the language paradigms created in that time such as Speakeasy, Simula, Smalltalk, and Prolog are still in use. What we need to know is that these paradigms became the stepping stones for the current programming languages.<br>\n<strong>Jacob: </strong> Jane, you really know about this. Thank you!<br>\n<strong>Jane: </strong> No problem! Anytime.", "image": "enpe7n4le01ob03re2img03.jpg", "id": 2}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n4le01ob03re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}]}